  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday) \nIssued at 625 AM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, a 500 mb ridge will be \ncentered over the southeastern CONUS, with troughing over the \nWest. Initially, a shortwave will be lifting northeast along the \nperiphery of this ridge, with another shortwave following closely \nbehind. By Tuesday evening, these waves will merge, and this \nenergy will work to fully flatten the ridge. Flow will become \nsouthwesterly aloft, but this pattern change will be relatively \nshort lived. Weak ridging attempts to build back into the area \nfor the end of the workweek before a better defined disturbance  \napproaches the forecast area later this weekend and brings about\nthe area's next meaningful chances for rainfall.\n\nOverall, the weather looks to be very uneventful through the end \nof the workweek. A frontal boundary previously stalled over Ohio \nwill also lift north as a warm front as Tuesday night's shortwave\ntrough passes by. This should not significantly affect Eastern \nKentucky's weather aside from an increase in cloud cover. This \nwill keep lows warmer than in previous days and bring an end to \nthe patchy frost potential that our area's hollows have \nexperienced as of late. Valley fog formation is still expected \nnear the area's waterways though. Otherwise, no impactful weather \nis expected... only ample sunshine. Highs will remain above \naverage but pleasant, with temperatures rising into the low to mid\n70s each day and lows in the 40s. Lows will occur in accordance \nwith our area's typical ridge/valley temperature splits, with \nridgetops in the upper 40s and valleys in the lower 30s.\n\nA more notable pattern shift looks to arrive next weekend as a \nmore well defined shortwave pushes its way northeast toward the \nforecast area. A moist air mass will settle over eastern Kentucky,\nwith LREF mean PW values increasing to around 1\" by late \nSaturday. Around this time, PoPs begin to tick up. Nonetheless, \nthe vast majority of LREF members continue to keep Halloween \nevening dry, with higher confidence in rain once the calendars\nswitch to November and the clocks fall back on Sunday morning.\nLREF mean PWAT values rise further towards the 1.2-1.3\" range by \nthen. While trends related to the timing of this system will need\nto be monitored closely in the coming days, these PWATs are not\nparticularly impressive. Detrimental hydrological impacts appear \nunlikely, and impacts will likely be determined by timing. If the\nrain begins on Halloween proper, it could be a trick... Nobody \nwants to get wet while enjoying fall festivities! If the rain \nholds off until after midnight, it would be a treat... a light \nrainfall would help mitigate fire weather and drought concerns\nheaded into the new month! So, stay tuned!",
